    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d638">

                <pb facs="0698" n="603" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg603"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.659 Matches/12: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-03-28T12:00:00-05:00">March 28, 1936—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>67. Nanking’s despatches Nos. 98<anchor n="21" xml:id="d638-f1"/> and 103 of
                    February 21 and 27 in regard to proposed match monopoly. On the basis of the
                    information contained in the Embassy’s despatches, the Department is inclined to
                    concur in the view expressed in the penultimate paragraph of Nanking’s No. 98
                    under reference.<anchor n="22" xml:id="d638-f2"/> In this connection, the
                    Embassy should bear in mind Department’s instruction No. 1745 of September 4,
                        1935,<anchor n="23" xml:id="d638-f3"/> on the subject of monopolies.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d638-f1" n="21" xml:id="d638-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d638-f2" n="22" xml:id="d638-f2-content">
                <p>The Ambassador in China stated that it was his intention “to take no further step
                    in connection with the discussions relating to the formation of a match monopoly
                    unless it shall seem necessary to emphasize further the opposition of the
                    American Government to monopolies.” (893.659 Matches/11)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d638-f3" n="23" xml:id="d638-f3-content">
                <p><ref target="frus1935v03#pg_786"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1935, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, p. 786</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
